== The Ancient Law and Unique Bond of Marriage

=== §. 79. #emph[Of the ancient Law of mariage.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[EPHES. 5. 31.]

    #emph[For this cause shall a man leaue his father and mother, and shall be ioyned vnto his wife: and they two shall be one flesh.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

THe same points which were before laid downe, concerning the neere vnion of man and wife, and of Christ and the Church, are here further confirmed by the ancient law of mariage: which the Apostle doth the rather mention, because it followeth vpon that text, whereunto he alluded in the former verse. For when #emph[Moses] had alledged these words of #emph[Adam] concerning #emph[Eue, This is bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh,] he #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 23, 24.] addeth this law, #emph[Therefore shall a man leaue his father,] &c. In this place these words haue both a #emph[literall] and a #emph[mysticall] sense. A #emph[literall] of man and wife. A #emph[mysticall] of Christ and the Church. The maine thing which the Apostle aimeth at, is to #footnote[Inference.] shew how neerely man and wife are linked together: that thereby they may the rathet be moued to performe those mutuall and seuerall duties which they owe each to other. But because he propounded to husbands and wiues the examples of Christ and the Church, as patternes and motiues to them, to doe their dutie, he applieth that which was first spoken of man and wife, vnto Christ and his Church, to shew that there being so fit a resemblance betwixt these two couples, the patterne propounded is the more pertinent to the purpose, and the reason enforced from thence the more forcible.

Because the opening of the literall sense will giue great light to the mysterie, I will first handle this text according to the meaning of the letter.

The first clause #emph[(for this cause)] implieth a necessarie connexion with that which went before. The neere vnion of man and wife, as well as of Christ and his Church, was before noted. A wife was said to be as the #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 28. #emph[Gen.] 2. 23.] #emph[bodie] of a man, yea as #footnote[#emph[Vers.] 28. #emph[Gen.] 2. 23.] #emph[himselfe. Adam] called her his #emph[flesh and bones.] Hereupon both #emph[Moses] and #emph[Paul] inferre, #emph[Therefore,] or, #emph[For this cause shall a man leaue father.] Because man and wife are so neere by Gods institution, they must also be most deare each to other in their mutuall affection.

The #emph[Man] (meaning an husband) is here in particular mentioned, #footnote[Meaning of the words.] because at the first making of this Law the woman was brought to him to see how he would like her: and hauing cast his affection on her, he was to be bound hereby to continue that good liking towards her: as also because of the preheminencie which man hath aboue his wife. Yet is not the man only tied hereby, but the wife also: the nature and rule of relation requireth as much: if a man must inseparably cleaue to his wife, the wife must answerably cleaue to her husband.

These words #emph[(shall leaue father and mother)] are neither #emph[generally] to be taken of all duties, as if no dutie were to be performed to parents by children after they are maried: nor #emph[simply,] as if indeed parents were vtterly to be forsaken: but they are meant,

1. Of that #emph[daily seruice] which children vnder their parents gouernment performe vnto them, seeking to please them in all things. When children are maried, then their daily attendance must be vpon their wiues, or husbands, taking care how #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 7. 33, 34.] to please them.

2. Of #emph[erecting a new family:] for which end their parents house must be left, and the husband and wife must dwell each with other.

3. Of the #emph[difference to be put betwixt parents, and wife or husband.] So as if by any ineuitable occasion it should so fall out, that a man must leaue his parent, or his wife (as in case parent and wife were both giuing vp the Ghost, and in places so farre remote, as the husband could not possibly be with both, yet both instantly desired his companie) by this Law he must leaue his parent, and cleaue to his wife.

Hereby then the bond of mariage is declared to be the most inuiolable bond that can be. For all men know, that the bond betwixt parent and childe is a firme and inuiolable bond: but the bond betwixt husband and wife is more firme and inuiolable.

To set forth the firmnesse of the mariage bond he addeth this Emphacicall phrase, #emph[shall be ioyned,] (or as the word properly, cording to the naturall notation thereof signifieth, #emph[shall be glued)] #footnote[Glue.] to his wife. Things well glued together are as fast, firme, and close as if they were one intire peece. Yea we obserue by experience that a table will oft times cleaue in the whole wood, before it will part asunder where it is glued: so as an husband ought to be as firme to his wife as to himselfe: and she to him.

Fitly doth this agree with that which followeth #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] Our English cannot well expresse the Greeke in good sense word for word (which is thus, #emph[they two shall beinto,] or #emph[in one flesh)] the meaning is, They which were two before mariage, by the bond of mariage are brought into one flesh, to be euen as one flesh: as neerely vnited, as the parts of the same body, and the same flesh. This vnitie is not in regard of carnall copulation (for if they be maried they are #emph[one flesh,] #footnote[#emph[Matrimonium non facit coitus, sed voluntas. Chrysost. in Mat. hom.] 32.] though they neuer know one another) nor in regard of procreation, because one childe commeth from them both (for though they neuer haue childe, yet are they one flesh) but in regard of Gods institution, who hath set it downe for a law, and as another nature, that man and wife should be so neere one to another. Their consent in mariage (by vertue of Gods institution) maketh them to be #emph[one flesh.]

Well doth our English note the emphasis of the originall in this particle THEY #emph[(they two)] which sheweth that the bond of mariage knitteth only #emph[two] together: one man, and one woman, and no more.

This Law setteth forth the #emph[Vnion betwixt man and wife.] #footnote[Summe.]

Therein three things are noted concerning the state of mariage.

1. The praeeminencie of it #emph[(a man shall leaue father and mother.)]

2. The firmnesse of it #emph[(and be ioyned to his wife.)]

3. The neerenesse of it #emph[(they two shall be one flesh.)]

=== §. 80. #emph[Of preferring husband or wife before parents.]

The first point sheweth, that

#emph[A wife, or an husband must be preferred before parents.] The #footnote[Doct.] examples of #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 31. 14.] #emph[Leah] and #emph[Rachel,] yea and of #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 19. 11] #emph[Micol,] are commendable in this respect.

1. The bond of mariage is more ancient, more firme, more #footnote[Reason.] neere. There was husband and wife before there was parent and child: and there is a time when parents & children may depart one from another, and that while both liue: but no time, wherein man and wife may part asunder till death part them. And children though they come from the flesh of their parents, yet are made two (so as #emph[of one are two)] but husbands and wiues though they were two before, yet are made one (so as #emph[of two is one.)]

What wrong then doe such parents vnto their children, as #footnote[Vse.] keepe them, euen after they are maried, so strait vnder subiection, #footnote[Parents may not keepe their children being maried too straight.] as they cannot freely performe such duty as they ought to their husband, or their wife? This is more then a parents authority reacheth vnto. Yet many thinke that their children owe as much seruice to them after they are maried as before: which is directly against this law.

Greater is the wrong, and more sinfull is the practise of #footnote[Nor keepe them from husband or wife.] such as keepe their children from their husbands, or from their wiues. The match (say they) falleth out much worse then we looked for. But this should haue beene looked to more carefully before hand. After mariage it is too late to seeke such a redresse.

On the other side, there be many children who so respect #footnote[Children may not preferre their parents before husband or wife. #emph[Apud Romanos sponsa obtecta flammeo capite quum extulisset pedem è paternis aedibus, vertebatur in gyrum, itaque ducebatur in domumsponsi, nesciret redeundi viam ad aedes parentum. Erasm.]] their parents, as they neglect their husband or their wife. Some husbands will bestow what they can on their parents, and keepe their wiues very bare, suffering them to want necessaries; not caring how they vex and grieue them so they please their parents. Some wiues also will priuily purloine from their husbands to bestow on their parents.

Others can neuer tarrie out of their parents houses, but as oft as they can, goe thither. The ancient Romans, to shew how vnmeet this was, had a custome to couer the brides face with a yellow veile, and so soone as she was out of her fathers house to turne her about and about, and so to carrie her to the house of her husband, that she might not know the way to her fathers house againe. All those pretenses of loue to parents are more preposterous then pious: and naturall affection beareth more sway in such, then true religion. Their pretence of piety to parents is no rust excuse for that iniury they doe to husband and wife.

=== §. 81. #emph[Of the firmnesse of the matrimoniall bond.]

The second point concerning the firmnesse of the mariage knot in these words #emph[(shall be ioyned to his wife)] affor deth two doctrines.

1. #emph[Man and wife must associate themselues together by continuall] #footnote[See #emph[Treat.] 2. #emph[part.] 2. §. 14.] #emph[cohabitation:] for this end they leaue their parents family, and erect a new family.

2. #emph[Man and wife are ioyned together by an inuiolable bond.] It #footnote[See #emph[Treat.] 2. #emph[part.] 2 §. 2.] must neuer be cut asunder till death cut it. Body and soule must be seuered one from another before husband and wife.

Be carefull therefore to preserue this indissoluble knot: and so liue together, as with comfort you may liue together, because you may not part.

=== §. 82. #emph[Of two only to be ioyned together in mariage.]

The third point concerning the neerenesse of man and wife, in these words #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] affordeth two other doctrines.

1. #emph[Mariage can be but betwixt two,] one man, and one woman: for it is impossible that more then two should so neerely, and firmely be ioyned together, as man and wife are. Euery word almost in this law proueth this doctrine. For it saith #emph[a man,] not men: to #emph[a wife,] not to wiues: to #emph[his] wife, not to anothers wife: #emph[two,] not more then two: #emph[they] two, not any two: #emph[one flesh,] not many fleshes.

#strong[#emph[Obiect.] This particle #emph[(two)] is not in the law as #emph[Moses] recordeth #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 2. 24.] it.]

#emph[Answ.] It is there necessarily implied, for at that time there were but two in the world: God then speaking of them, meaneth but two. The same spirit that guided #emph[Moses,] guided also the #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 5.] Euangelists, and the #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 16.] Apostles: so as by their inserting of this particle #emph[(two)] it is certaine that it was intended by #emph[Moses:] as the particle #emph[(only)] which Christ putteth into this text, #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 6. 13. #emph[Mat.] 4. 10.] #emph[him only shalt thou serue.]

#strong[#emph[Quest.] Why did God at first make but one man, and one woman?]

#emph[Answ.] The #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 15.] Prophet answereth, #emph[that he might seeke a godly seed.]

If therefore there be more then two, it is an adulterous seed which proceedeth from thence.

=== §. 83. #emph[Of Polygamy and Bigamy.]

Can Polygamy (the hauing of many wiues) or Bigamy (the hauing of two wiues at once) haue any good warrant against such an expresse law? Are not both of them against the first institution of mariage, so as we may say, #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 8.] #emph[from the beginning it was not so?] Yea also and against #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 17. 17. #emph[Leu.] 18. 18.] other particular lawes? #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 4. 19. #emph[Magis pertinere ad nuptiarum bonum, non vnum & multas, sed vnum & vnam, satis indicat ipsa prima diuinitus facta coniugum copula, &c. Aug. de Nupt. & concup. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9. #emph[Gen.] 2. 18. #emph[Gen.] 16. 5. & 21. 11.] #emph[Lamech] one of #emph[Cains] cursed stocke was the first that we read of to haue presumed against that ancient law.

#strong[#emph[Obiect.] Afterwards many Patriarkes, and other Saints tooke that liberty vnto themselues.]

#emph[Answ.] It was their sinne, and a great blemish in them. The common error of the time, & their vnsatiable desire of increase made them fall into it. Many inconueniences followed thereupon: neither can it be thought but that much mischiefe must needes follow vpon hauing more wiues then one: for whereas God at first made a wife to be as an helpe vnto man, two, or more wiues cannot but be a great griefe and vexation vnto him by reason of that emulation that is betwixt them. Through #emph[Hagars] meanes was #emph[Sarah] stirred against #emph[Abraham,] and #emph[Abraham] grieued at #emph[Sarahs] words. Though #emph[Leah] and #emph[Rachel] were sisters, yet great were their emulations: the like whereof is noted of #emph[Peninnah] and many others.

Considering the hainousnesse of this sinne, our lawes #footnote[#emph[Stat.] 1. #emph[Iacob.] 11.] haue iustly made it felony for a man to haue more wiues then one, or a woman more husbands.

=== §. 84. #emph[Of the neere coniunction of man and wife together.]

2. #emph[The neerest of all other are husband and wife one to another.] #footnote[Doctr.] Euery clause in the forenamed law proueth as much.

1. #emph[Parents] must be #emph[left] for wife: who neerer then parent and childe? if man and wife be neerer then the neerest, then they are the neerest of all.

2. A man is #emph[glued] to his wife. This metaphor setteth forth the #emph[neernesse] of a thing as well as the #emph[firmnesse] of it: for things glued together are as one intire thing.

3. Man and wife are #emph[one flesh:] many of one are made two, but no #emph[two] so neerely and truly made #emph[one] as man and wife.

As God hath limited a propinquity, and vnity of things, so #footnote[Reason.] are they to be accounted: but God hath thus neerely knit man and wife together, and made them one flesh. #emph[Those whom] #footnote[#emph[Mat.] 19. 6.] GOD #emph[hath ioyned together,] saith Christ of man and wife: in which respect matrimoniall coniunction is called #emph[the couenant] #footnote[#emph[Pro.] 2. 17.] #emph[of God:] so as this couenant cannot be released by any, no not by the mutuall consent of man and wife #emph[(Those whom] GOD #emph[hath ioyned together, let no man put asunder)] yet may many other couenants made betwixt partie and partie, be released and disanulled by mutuall consent of both parties.

1. This sheweth that the transgressions of man and wife #footnote[Vses.] one against another are of all the most hainous, more then of #footnote[1\. Mutuall transgressions of man and wife most hainous.] friend, fellow, brother, childe, parent or any other. Who would not cry #emph[fie vpon] that child that hates his parent, or #emph[fie vpon] that parent that hates his childe? The heathen & sauages would not thinke them worthy of humane society. What then may be thought of the man that hateth his wife, or the wife that hateth her husband? Apply this to all other transgressions: and well note how #emph[the Lord is a witnesse] thereof. #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 14.]

2. This also sheweth how monstrous a thing it is to sow #footnote[2\. Monstrous to make debate betwixt man and wife.] any seeds of discord, and stirre debate betwixt man and wife. The deuils instruments they are therein, and a diabolicall spirit is in them. For Satan most laboureth to vnloose those knots which the Lord knitteth most firmly. Children of seuerall venters, and seuerall friends of each partie, are much faultie herein. #emph[Cursed be they all before the Lord.]

3. #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 26. 19.] This neere coniunction betwixt man and wife is a great #footnote[3\. All duties betwixt man and wife to be cheerefully performed. #emph[Isa.] 58. 7.] motiue to stirre them both vp, cheerefully to performe all the duties which God requireth of either of them. For thereby they doe dutie, and shew kindnesse to their owne flesh. No man may #emph[hide himselfe from his owne flesh] at large: that is, no man may neglect any dutie of mercie, or iustice to his neighbour who is of the same stocke that he is: shall then an husband or wife hide themselues from one another who in the neerest respect that possibly can be are #emph[one flesh?] not because they come #emph[from] one flesh, but because they come #emph[into] one flesh.

Hitherto of the literall sense of this verse.

The mysticall followeth.

=== §. 85. #emph[Of the matrimoniall coniunction of Christ and the Church.]

The forenamed ancient mariage law is here applied mystically #footnote[#emph[Quod per historiam impletum est in Adam, per prophetiam significat Christum qui reliquit patrem, &c. Aug. de Gen. lib.] 2. #emph[c.] 24.] to Christ and the Church, as is euident by the next verse, where the Apostle hauing reference to this verse saith, #emph[This is a great mysterie.] There is then a mysterie contained in it. But of what, or of whom is that mysterie? The Apostle himselfe maketh answer, in these words: #emph[I speake concerning Christ and the Church.]

The mysterie in generall is this,

#emph[Christ and the Church are to one another as husband and wife.]

The particulars of this mysterie are these.

The matrimoniall coniunction betwixt Christ & the Church is a most

+ Preheminent
+ Firme
+ Neere

coniunction.

First of the generall.

The many espousall, and matrimoniall titles, which in Scripture are giuen to Christ and the Church in mutuall relation of one to another, euidently shew that they are ioyned together by the honourable, inseparable and inuiolable bond of mariage: He is stiled a #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 3. 29.] #emph[Bridegroome,] she a #emph[Bride:] he #footnote[#emph[Cant.] 1. 13, 15.] #emph[Welbeloued,] she #emph[Loue:] he an #footnote[2 #emph[Cor.] 11. 2.] #emph[Husband,] she a #emph[Wife:] he an #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 23.] #emph[Head,] she the #emph[Body:] both #footnote[#emph[& vers.] 31.] #emph[one flesh.]

2. All things requisite to ioyne man and wife together, doe fitly concurre betwixt Christ and the Church.

1. They are persons fit to be ioyned. Though Christ be #footnote[the things which make Christ and the Church fit to be husband and wife.] God, yet for #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 17. 19.] this end he became man: and though the Church were impure, yet for this end is she cleansed and sanctified.

2. They haue their parents consent: for God is the #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 20. 17.] common father of both. And God hath giuen #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 8. 32.] Christ to the Church, and the #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 6. 39.] Church to Christ.

3. They haue giuen their #footnote[#emph[Cant.] 2. 16.] mutuall consent each to other.

4. He #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 5. 23, 24.] beareth an husband like affection to her, and she is willing to yeeld a wife-like subiection to him.

5. He hath #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 4 8.] giuen her many fauours and gifts as pledges of his loue: and she in testimony of her faithfulnesse was vnder the Law circumcised, and is vnder the Gospell baptized: and doth binde her selfe with all the sacred bonds and couenants which God to that purpose hath sanctified.

6. He hath prepared #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 14. 3.] places of habitation for them both together, and #footnote[#emph[Reu.] 22. 17, 20.] she earnestly desireth to be with him.

Behold another euidence of Christs admirable loue to the #footnote[1\. #emph[Vse.]] Church, and of the neere vnion betwixt Christ and her. The #footnote[An high fauour to be the spouse to Christ.] former was that she was his #emph[body.] This, that she is his #emph[wife:] well might the Church say as #emph[Abigaile] did, #emph[Behold, let thine handmaid be a seruant to wash the feet of the seruants of my] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 25. 41.] #emph[Lord:] and as the prodigall child, #emph[Make me as one of thine hired] #footnote[#emph[Luk.] 15. 19.] #emph[seruants:] or as the Baptist, #emph[I am not worthy to stoope downe to] #footnote[#emph[Mar.] 1. 7.] #emph[vnloose thy shoe-latchets.] What a fauour then is it to be made his spouse, his wife, his Queene. Great was the fauour which #emph[Ahash-verosh] shewed to #emph[Esther,] when he made her his wife: he was a great Monarch, reigning #emph[from India to Ethiopia ouer] 127 #emph[prouinces:] but #emph[Esther] was a poore orphane and captiue: yet was not this fauour comparable to Christs: for there was no such disparity and inequality betwixt #emph[Ahash-verosh] and #emph[Esther,] as betwixt Christ and the Church: neither is #emph[Esthers] aduancement to be compared with the Churches: and yet there was some cause in #emph[Esther] to moue #emph[Ahash-verosh] to doe what he did, for she was very beautifull, and louely, and worthy to be loued: but #footnote[§. 33, 34.] in the Church when Christ first cast his loue on her, there was no such thing. No patterne of loue can be giuen any way comparable to this.

Let the Church therefore, and all that professe themselues #footnote[2\. #emph[Vse.]] to be of the Church, take such notice hereof, as they may endeuour #footnote[#emph[Direction.] How Christs spouse must cary her selfe.] to carry themselues worthy of this honour and aduancement: not to wax proud and insolent thereupon, but to despise all vaine and worldly toies: to answer loue with loue, as the Church is set forth in #emph[Salomons] song; to be subiect to her husband, to reuerence and obey him, and to performe all duties appertaining to such a wife: seeking by all good meanes to maintaine the honour of her place. The Church is made a patterne of dutie to all wiues: if she should faile, greater inconuenience would follow from thence, then from #emph[Vashties] #footnote[#emph[Est.] 1. 16. #emph[&c.]] disobedience.

This is the rather to be regarded because it is not only a #footnote[3\. #emph[Vse.]] matter of instruction but of triall also, shewing both what they #footnote[#emph[Triall.] Who are Christs spouse.] which are of the Church ought to doe, and also #footnote[§. 24.] what indeed they will doe. Wherefore no prophane person that lightly esteemeth the Lord Iesus, no Idolater that casteth his loue on other husbands, no swearer or blasphemer that dishonoreth the great Name of Iesus, none that any way are rebellious against him, none that hate, scorne, scoffe, or hurt any of his members, can haue any comfort in this aduancement of the Church, because they haue no part therein, nor right thereunto.

But great is that comfort which the true Saints may receiue #footnote[4\. #emph[Vse.]] therein. For by vertue of this matrimoniall bond, #footnote[#emph[Consolation.] The priuiledges of Christs spouse.]

1. Christ is made a yoakefellow with his Church, and her companion. Vnder all the burdens which are laid vpon her, he putteth his shoulder to make it the more easie: yea, the great burdens of Gods wrath, the curse of the law, and sinne the cause thereof, hath he so taken on him, as he hath cleane freed his Church from them, because they would else haue crushed her downe to hell.

2. Christ is as her champion to answer all challenges sent vnto her, as her aduocate to plead and answer all the complaints that shall be made against her, as her suretie to discharge all her debts: the Church being couert-baron vnder Christ, he is as her selfe, all in all for her, and to her.

3. All his honours, goods, priuiledges are hers: she hath #footnote[#emph[Ecclesia omnem sponsi potestatem tenet. Aug. contr. Don. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 1.] a right to them, and her part in them, she is a coheire with him #emph[(Rom.] 8. 17.) a Queene because he a King #emph[(Psal.] 45. 9.) and all glorious, as was noted, vers. 27.

4. He will assuredly performe all the offices of an husband, as to loue her, beare with her, prouide for her, with the like. Able he is to doe all, for he is omnipotent: willing also he must needs be, because willingly he hath taken vpon him this place: he hath made himselfe a patterne to other husbands: will he not then doe that himselfe which herequireth of others?

If euer any wife might receiue comfort in a match, the Church may receiue comfort in this match.

The benefit of this match will yet more liuely appeare by a particular consideration of the three forenamed properties of this matrimoniall bond, the #emph[preheminencie, firmnesse,] and #emph[neerenesse] thereof.

=== §. 86. #emph[Of Christs leauing his Father and mother for his spouse.]

I. The preheminencie of the matrimoniall bond betwixt Christ and the Church herein appeareth, that

#emph[Christ left his Father and his mother for his spouse the Church.] As Christ is God, God is his Father; as man, the Virgin #emph[Marie] was his mother. Now the leauing of his Father must be taken only by way of resemblance, in that he came from the place of his Fathers habitation, to the place where his Spouse was. The Scripture saith, that he #emph[was in the bosome of his Father:] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 1. 18.] #emph[by him, as one brought vp with him, his daily delight, reioycing alway] #footnote[#emph[Prou.] 8. 30.] #emph[before him:] yet #emph[descended he into the lowest parts of the earth] #footnote[#emph[Ephes.] 4. 9.] #emph[where his spouse was. He came out from the Father, and came into] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 16. 28.] #emph[the world.]

But truly and properly did he preferre his Spouse before his mother. For when he was instructing his Spouse, and his mother came to interrupt him, he said to his mother, #emph[who is my] #footnote[#emph[Mark.] 3. 33, 34.] #emph[mother?] and to his Spouse, #emph[behold my mother.]

Of the #footnote[Vse.] same minde must the Church, and all that are of the Church be vnto Christ: she must #emph[forget her owne people, and fathers] #footnote[#emph[Imitation.] All to be forsaken for Christ. #emph[Psal.] 45. 10.] #emph[house.] Seeing Christ hath gone before vs, and giuen vs so good an example, what an high point of ingratitude would it be for vs, to preferre father, mother, or any other before Christ our husband? Note what he saith in this case, #emph[He that loueth] #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 10. 37.] #emph[father or mother more then me, is not worthie of me.] And againe, #emph[If any come vnto me, and hate not his father and mother, he cannot] #footnote[#emph[Luke] 14. 26. expounded. How parents to be hated for Christ.] #emph[be mine.] To #emph[hate] here, is to be so farre from preferring father & mother before Christ, as rather then not to loue Christ, to hate father and mother. Or, so intirely to loue Christ aboue all, as our loue of parents in comparison thereof to be an hatted. Thus #emph[Leui said vnto his father and mother, I haue not seene him:] #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 33. 9.] #emph[for they obserued the word, and kept the couenant of Christ.]

This then is our dutie, that we suffer not any naturall affection and dotage on our parents to swallow vp that loue we owe to Christ, as #emph[Pharaohs ill-fauourèd and leane-fleshed kine eat] #footnote[#emph[Gen.] 41. 4.] #emph[vp the seuen well-fauoured and fat kine.] How much lesse should any loue of this world, of the profits, promotions, or pleasures of this world, draw away our hearts from Christ; should we not rather say and doe as the Apostles did, #emph[Behold, we haue for saken] #footnote[#emph[Matth.] 19. 27.] #emph[all and followed Christ?]

=== §. 87. #emph[Of the indissoluble vnion betwixt Christ and the Church.]

II. The firmnesse of that bond whereby Christ and the Church are said to be #emph[glued] together, is greater and more inuiolable then that whereby man and wife are ioyned together: Death parteth man and wife: but death cannot make a diremption betwixt Christ and the Church: so as we may well from this metaphor inferre, that #emph[Christ and the Church are inseparably] #footnote[#emph[Hosea] 2. 19.] #emph[knit together. I will betroth thee vnto me for euer,] saith Christ vnto the Church. The couenant which Christ maketh with his Church, is an #emph[euerlasting] couenant. #emph[The mountaines shall depart,] #footnote[#emph[Isa.] 61. 8. #emph[&] 54. 10.] #emph[and the hils be remoued, before his kindnesse shall depart from the Church.]

The stedfastnesse and vnchangeablenesse of his will, is the #footnote[Reason.] only cause thereof. Whom he loueth, #emph[he loueth vnto the end.] #footnote[#emph[Ioh.] 13. 1.] #emph[His gifts and calling are without repentance.] He is not like the #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 11. 29.] hard hearted Iewes, who vpon euerie sleight occasion would put away their wiues. #emph[The Lord hateth putting away.] Though #footnote[#emph[Mal.] 2. 16.] therefore the Church, through her weaknesse, doe depart from him, and play the harlot, #emph[yet returne againe to me,] saith the #footnote[#emph[Ier.] 3. 1.] Lord.

Learne we by this patterne to cleaue close vnto the Lord, #footnote[#emph[Vse.] Cleaue to Christ.] which is a dutie most due to Christ who cleaueth so close to vs, and therefore oft #footnote[#emph[Deut.] 10. 20. #emph[&] 13. 4. #emph[Acts] 11. 23.] expressed in the Scripture.

Three vertues there are which are of speciall vse to this purpose, Faith, Hope, Loue.

#emph[Faith] is the hand whereby we lay fast hold on Christ, and as it were knit him to our selues, as he by his Spirit knitteth vs to himselfe. This maketh vs rest and repose our selues on him for all needfull things: and not to leaue him for any thing.

#emph[Hope] is the anchor, which holdeth vs fast against all the stormes of Satan, so as they can neuer driue vs out of our harbour, which is the Lord Iesus Christ.

#emph[Loue] is the glue and soader which maketh vs one with Christ: for it is the propertie of loue to vnite those that loue one another in one. #emph[Ionathans soule was knit with the soule of] #footnote[1 #emph[Sam.] 18. 1.] #emph[Dauid.] For why? #emph[Ionathan loued him as his owne soule.] He that loueth is well pleased with him whom he loueth, and seeketh also to please him, that they may mutually delight one in another. Were these three vertues well rooted in vs, we would say, #emph[who shall separate vs from the loue of Christ? shall tribulation,] #footnote[#emph[Rom.] 8. 35. #emph[&c.]] #emph[or distresse,] &c.

=== §. 88. #emph[Of the equall priuiledge of all the Saints.]

III. Concerning the phrase, whereby the neerenesse of #footnote[All the Saints made one Spouse.] man and wife is set forth #emph[(they two shall be one flesh)] it may be demanded how this can be applied to Christ and the Saints, who are more then two?

#emph[Answ.] Christ by one Spirit knitteth vs all into one bodie, #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 12. 13.] and so maketh all ioyntly considered together one Spouse. The multitude of Saints doth no more imply many wiues, then the multitude of members which the naturall bodie of a wife hath. This point then teacheth vs, that

#emph[In the mysticall mariage betwixt Christ and the Church, all and euerie of the Saints haue an equall priuiledge.] Some are not #footnote[See §. 22.] Concubines, some wiues, nor some more loued, or preferred to another, but all one wife. #emph[All are one in Christ Iesus.] #footnote[#emph[Gal.] 3. 28.]

Neither the Father that gaue them all, nor the Sonne who #footnote[Reason.] tooke them all, saw any thing in one more then in another; their meere grace moued them to doe what they did. Well may euerie one apply all the forenamed priuiledges vnto themselues: and not one emulate another.

This affordeth instruction to the more eminent in the #footnote[Vse.] Church, that like proud dames they insult not ouer others, as if they were their hand-maids: and consolation to the meaner sort, that they may vphold themselues, and possesse their soules with patience, and not enuie, or grieue at the outward prosperitie and priuiledges of others. In the greatest priuiledge they are equall to the greatest.

This of the parties coupled to Christ. For these words #emph[(they two)] shew that all the Saints are but #emph[one:] Christ is the other of the two. The next words #emph[(are one flesh)] shew how neere those Saints are to Christ.

=== §. 89. #emph[Of the neere vnion betwixt Christ and the Church.]

The maine point here to be noted is, that

#emph[Christ and the Church are most neerely linked together.] What #footnote[#emph[Christus nihil aliud deputat corpus suum (i.) Ecclesiam, quam seipsum, quia de Christo & ecclesia verius intelligitur, Erunt duo in carne una. Aug. de pec. mer. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 31.] can be neerer, then that two should come #emph[into one flesh?]

This is somewhat more then to be #emph[of Christs flesh.] That shewes we are as it were cut out of Christ: this shewes that we are againe knit to him. That was a preparation vnto this: this is as the consummation and perfection of all. #footnote[See §. 70.] Many metaphors are vsed to set forth the neere vnion betwixt Christ and his Church, but this surpasseth them all. As here we and Christ are said to be #emph[one flesh,] so in another place, #emph[one spirit.] Well therefore might the name and title #emph[Christ] be giuen to this Spouse of Christ.

#footnote[§. 84.] It was noted on a like ground to this, that of all other persons #footnote[1 #emph[Cor.] 6. 17. #emph[&] 12. 12.] the transgression of a wife against an husband is most hainous. What then are the transgressions of the Church against Christ? As we are much more bound vnto Christ for the priuiledges we receiue from him as an #emph[Head] and #emph[Husband,] and so our Sauiour hauing made with vs an euerlasting couenant of mariage, then for those we receiued from him as our Creator, Lord and Master: so are the rebellions now committed against him more monstrous. To #emph[Adam] that broke the first couenant whereby like a rebellious childe and seruant he sinned against his Father and Master, mercy and pardon was giuen: but to such as now breake the bond of this euerlasting couenant, and make a totall and finall desertion, vtterly renouncing this Husband, or by their adulterie cause him to giue them a bill of diuorce, #emph[there remaineth no more sacrifice] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 10. 27.] #emph[for sinnes; but a certaine fearfull looking for of iudgement, and fierie indignation which shall deuoure the aduersaries.] This is to be noted, to make vs the more circumspect ouer our waies, resisting sinne in the beginning, and #emph[looking diligently lest any] #footnote[#emph[Heb.] 12. 15.] #emph[man fall from the grace of God;] and #emph[giuing no place at all vnto] #footnote[#emph[Eph.] 4. 27.] #emph[the Deuill.] Satan will most endeuour to dissolue the neerest bonds that God maketh. This then being the neerest of all, we ought to be the most carefull in preseruing it.
